17592186045417] [17592186083433 #edb/ref 1045 "Introduction to the Elimination Method" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083433 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186084021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083433 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083433 #edb/ref 1050 780.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186083433 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "a68b3f7f-449b-5dcc-876b-2a9dda0b7af9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084021 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084021 #edb/ref 1061 143346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084021 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "22b878ba-aa9a-5218-889c-006c8dcacca7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084021 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084022 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084022 #edb/ref 1061 143347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084022 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5234accd-01b6-501e-866d-11b931397534" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084022 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084023 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084023 #edb/ref 1061 143351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084023 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "adba60f0-2f20-5985-8599-c86e9c30f9db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084023 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084024 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084025 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084024 #edb/ref 1061 143354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084024 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1c45a64c-2352-5510-9c77-6d975e32ee8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084024 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186084515 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084025 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084025 #edb/ref 1061 143355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084025 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f6f3680b-161b-5ba4-b393-bb9b5b36236b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084025 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084026 #edb/ref 1061 143359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084026 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b6308dc5-5a40-5abe-9f8e-91524b3b2359" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084026 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084514 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we want to solve, *without using graphs*, the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y &=1 \\\\ 5x-y &=11\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we must combine the equations into a single equation that can be solved using algebraic manipulation. One way to combine the equations is to use the **elimination method.** \n\nNotice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $1,$ and the coefficient of $y$ in the second equation is $-1.$ Since these numbers add to zero, we can *add* the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $y,$ and solve the resulting equation for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x+\\cancel{y} & =1 \\\\ 5x-\\cancel{y} & =11 \\\\ (x+5x) & =(1+11) \\\\ 6x & =12 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=2.$ However, we still need to find $y.$ To do this, we substitute $x=2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (2)+y & =1 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=2$ and $y=-1,$ which can be expressed as $(2, -1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186084514 #edb/ref 1065 8702 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084514 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "48f030ba-6728-565a-be53-cfb1e88abc86" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084514 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084515 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, suppose that we want to solve the following system of linear equations using elimination:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y &=1 \\\\ x-y &=-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAs it stands, adding the two equations will not eliminate any of the variables. \n\nHowever, suppose we want to eliminate the variable $x$. We begin by multiplying the second equation by $\\color{blue}-1$, which gives the following equivalent system:\n\n![](images/85/85996afab1889f8032a295f4db6bbbcbc8670a0bc12f0b64f53a3b809f111628.svg)\n\nThen, we add the two resulting equations together. This eliminates the variable $x,$ making it possible to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{x}+2y & =1 \\\\ -\\cancel{x}+y & =2 \\\\ (2y+y) & =(1+2) \\\\ 3y & =3 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=1.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x-y & =-2 \\\\ x-(1) & =-2 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-1$ and $y=1,$ which can be expressed as $(-1, 1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186084515 #edb/ref 1065 8704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084515 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "170be181-ae77-50d7-a5f1-0a4bdcfa318e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084515 #edb/ref 1067 "Eliminating Variables From Systems of Linear Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
